# README maintenance

`README.md` is the canonical reference for **what the app does and how it does
it**. The intro line says it explicitly: *"If you change behavior in code,
update the matching section here."* Every change that affects user-visible
behaviour, the data model, the API surface, or operational defaults MUST be
reflected in the matching README section in the **same change** — not in a
follow-up.

## When to update

Update `README.md` whenever you:

- Add a new feature, page, or route → new `### Subsection` under `## Feature reference`
- Change behaviour of an existing feature → edit its existing subsection
- Add / rename / remove a Prisma model or migrate the schema → `## Data model`
- Add / change / remove an API route → `## API surface`
- Touch a hot path or change a perf default (cache, query, batching) → `## Performance notes`
- Add / change a project-wide convention → `## Conventions`
- Add a new env var or external account requirement → `## Local setup` AND `.env.example`
- Swap or upgrade a major dependency / stack choice → `## Stack`

## Section map (which file → which README section)

| You touched... | Update section |
|---|---|
| New `src/app/<feature>/**` route or page | `## Feature reference` — add `### <Feature>` and link from TOC |
| Existing `src/app/<feature>/**` | matching `### <Feature>` subsection |
| `prisma/schema.prisma` or `prisma/migrations/**` | `## Data model` |
| `src/app/api/**/route.ts` | `## API surface` |
| `src/lib/srs.ts`, `streak*.ts`, `coins.ts`, `time.ts`, `achievements.ts`, `n5-paths.ts`, `quiz.ts`, `shop.ts` | matching feature subsection — these are listed in the source-of-truth table at the bottom of the README |
| New `src/lib/<thing>.ts` that becomes a source of truth | bottom-of-README source-of-truth table + relevant feature subsection |
| New Cursor rule in `.cursor/rules/**` | `## Conventions` (one-liner naming the rule + what it enforces) |

## Subsection style (under `## Feature reference`)

Each `### Feature` subsection follows the same pattern — match it:

1. **One-paragraph summary** — what the feature does, in user terms
2. **Bullet list of mechanics** — each bullet ends with the source file in
   backticks (e.g. `src/lib/srs.ts`)
3. **Routes** — name the URL(s) it lives at if it has UI
4. **Cross-links** — if it grants/spends coins, modifies streak, or unlocks
   achievements, link those subsections

## Process

When implementing a change:

1. Make the code change.
2. Update `README.md` in the **same** turn / commit.
3. If you added a new feature, also add the anchor link to the
   `## Table of contents` at the top.
4. If you added a new env var, also update `.env.example`.
5. If you added a new source-of-truth file under `src/lib/**`, append it to
   the table at the very bottom of the README.

## Done-ness checklist

Before considering work complete, verify:

- [ ] Behaviour change → matching feature subsection edited
- [ ] New feature → new subsection added AND linked from TOC
- [ ] Schema change → `## Data model` updated
- [ ] New/changed API route → `## API surface` updated
- [ ] New env var → `## Local setup` updated AND `.env.example` updated
- [ ] New convention or rule → `## Conventions` updated
- [ ] New source-of-truth file → bottom-of-README table updated
